Exactly. “Normal” is not merely descriptive; it can become an operator.

People constantly normalize through repetition:

\text{novel} \rightarrow \text{repeated} \rightarrow \text{familiar} \rightarrow \text{expected} \rightarrow \text{normal}

Once something reaches normal, behavior gets cheaper. It requires less explanation, attention, negotiation, and often less conscious effort. That’s a resource.

So you can deliberately steer the normalization process rather than merely being subjected to it.

A useful formulation is:

\boxed{\text{Normalize what you want to become cheap; denormalize what you want noticed.}}

That applies almost everywhere. Normalize exercise and it becomes routine rather than an event. Normalize documentation and organizational knowledge accumulates almost automatically. Normalize checking assumptions and skepticism becomes procedural. Normalize experimentation and failure becomes information rather than exceptional disruption.

But the reverse operator matters just as much. Denormalization restores information lost to familiarity. Ask why meetings are an hour long, why work happens five days a week, why a particular interface has that shape, why some process requires three approvals. Frequently the answer is not because it must but because it became normal.

There’s another layer: normalization changes the reference frame itself.

If R_t represents what feels normal at time t, repeated exposure updates it:

R_{t+1}=R_t+\alpha(x-R_t)

Then yesterday’s exceptional x gradually becomes tomorrow’s baseline R.

That makes normalization a kind of slow-moving reality compiler for individuals, organizations, cultures, and technologies.

And there’s a useful connection to homeostasis: once a behavior becomes part of the expected baseline, maintaining it can require less corrective effort than establishing it did. You haven’t merely performed the behavior repeatedly—you’ve changed the reference state against which deviation is perceived.

So I’d add a control loop:

Notice → Normalize deliberately → Harvest reduced friction → Audit → Denormalize temporarily → inspect residuals → decide what deserves renormalization.

The audit is crucial because the same machinery that turns excellent practices into effortless habits can turn terrible practices into invisible ones.

Habit is normalization at the behavioral scale. Culture is normalization at the group scale. Convention is normalization across populations. Paradigm is normalization at the level of thought.

Learning to move deliberately between those scales gives you quite a steering wheel.